analysisWe need to read the comment: "@@@ If fromIndex is greater than or equal to 0, then for every index i from fromIndex up to but not including the result, the bit at index i is set to true."

We need to produce an assert statement reflecting that.

Method nextClearBit returns the index of the next clear bit (false) from fromIndex inclusive. The comment says: if fromIndex >=0, then for every i in [fromIndex, result) (i.e., fromIndex <= i < result), the bit at i is set to true. That matches behavior: bits are true until a false found.

We need to write assertion: assert fromIndex >= 0 => (\forall int i; fromIndex <= i && i < \result; this.get(i)); but need to use observer method get(int). The method is in same class; we can call this.get(i). However we must ensure that we refer to result variable \result. Also need to handle case where result may be fromIndex (if that bit is clear). The condition "up to but not including the result" means that if result == fromIndex, the quantifier range is empty, trivially true.

Thus assert: assert fromIndex >= 0 => (\forall int i; i >= fromIndex && i < \result; this.get(i)); But also need that bits are true, i.e., get(i) == true. So we need this.get(i) == true, or just this.get(i). Since get returns boolean, we can just use this.get(i). In JML style, we can write this.get(i). So final: assert fromIndex >= 0 => (\forall int i; i >= fromIndex && i < \result; this.get(i)); Or using 0 <= fromIndex condition.

Better to include parentheses: assert fromIndex >= 0 => (\forall int i; i >= fromIndex && i < \result; this.get(i)); That's fine.

Make sure syntax: \forall int i; cond; spec. So cond is i >= fromIndex && i < \result. Spec is this.get(i). Since get returns boolean, we can use this.get(i). So final.

Wrap in <code> tags.

assistantfinal<code>assert fromIndex >= 0 => (\forall int i; i >= fromIndex && i < \result; this.get(i));</code>
